THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 proposes caching prior labels and recomputing only the affected subgraph after k bounded edits, emitting a certificate that the result is the least fixed point. But this proposal is opaque about what 'the affected subgraph' means under the skeptical fixed-point semantics of spec §4 Pass 1. In an attack-graph semantics where reinstatement is a derived property, a single bounded edge edit can propagate globally: a status change to one argument can reinstate or refute a distant argument through chains of attack and support, and removing one edge can un-suspend an argument that then attacks another far away. Thus there is no locally bounded subgraph whose relabeling suffices — recomputing only 'the affected' region risks missing global reinstatement effects and yielding a label assignment that is not the true fixed point, violating the determinism and validity-of-roots guarantees. The monotonicity assertion ('relabeling is monotone') is also unjustified: skeptical semantics involve cycles and non-monotone interactions where status changes oscillate, so the affected set is neither locally identifiable nor monotone. The certificate checks uniqueness but not whether the computation actually reached the correct fixpoint over the whole graph.

TARGET:
Since the variator emits k bounded edits per step and most of the attack graph is unchanged, cache the prior label assignment and recompute only the affected subgraph, emitting a certificate that the result is the least fixed point. Determinism is preserved because the certificate verifies uniqueness of the fixpoint regardless of iteration order; cost stays polynomial and amortizes better than full recomputation because the edit is bounded and relabeling is monotone. This targets the real cost center — the edges, not the fixpoint — keeping the extension cheap enough to run at every loop step.